4. Deleted terraform.tfstate file?
  The terraform.tfstate file is very important because Terraform uses it to keep track of the infrastructure resources it manages. 
If the terraform.tfstate file is deleted accidentally, Terraform will lose the record of the existing infrastructure. However, 
the actual resources in the cloud will not be deleted; only the state information is lost. Because of this, when we run terraform plan or terraform apply again, 
Terraform may think that no resources exist and it might try to create new resources, which can cause duplication or conflicts. To recover from this situation, 
first I check if there is a backup of the state file. Many teams store the state remotely using backends like AWS S3 with versioning enabled, 
so we can restore the previous version of the state file from the S3 bucket. If no backup is available, we can use the terraform import command to 
manually import the existing infrastructure resources back into Terraform state. This allows Terraform to rebuild the state file and continue managing the 
infrastructure safely.


5. Deleted Resource entry from Terraform.tfstate
 Terraform loses track of that specific resource, but the actual resource in the cloud still exists.
 Next time you run terraform plan or terraform apply, Terraform will think the resource does not exist 
 and may try to create a new resource, which can lead to duplicate resources.
 This can cause conflicts, especially if the resource has dependencies with other resources.
 If you have a backup of the state file (e.g., in a remote backend like S3, 
 restore the state file or the missing resource entry from the backup.
 If no backup is available, you can use terraform import to manually re-import the existing resource into the state file. For example:
 Example : terraform import aws_instance.my_instance i-0123456789abcdef0


