12. Manage EKS Cred

In my AWS projects, when working with Amazon EKS, I manage credentials securely using AWS IAM rather than hardcoding them. 
Each user, CI/CD pipeline, or pod is assigned an IAM role with the minimum permissions needed. For accessing the cluster, 
I generate temporary credentials using the AWS CLI or aws-iam-authenticator and update the kubeconfig with commands like 
aws eks update-kubeconfig --region <region> --name <cluster-name>. For pods that need AWS access, I use IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) 
so the pods can assume roles dynamically and get temporary credentials without storing secrets in code. This approach ensures secure, 
least-privilege access, works well with Terraform-managed EKS clusters, and allows CI/CD pipelines to interact with the cluster safely without exposing 
sensitive information.


13. Continous Delivery Vs Deployment
For example, in a project I worked on, our team used Jenkins pipelines for a web application. 
Every developer commit triggered automated build, unit tests, and integration tests. In Continuous Delivery, 
the pipeline would prepare the artifact and notify the team, and we would manually approve deployment to staging or production. 
In Continuous Deployment, after passing all tests, the artifact was automatically deployed to the staging and production environment 
using Docker and Kubernetes, without any manual approval. This allowed us to release new features quickly while keeping production stable.


