15. RollBack and Rollout?
We deploy our application on Kubernetes using rolling updates. 
When a new version is released, Jenkins updates the container image in the Kubernetes deployment. 
Kubernetes gradually replaces old pods with new pods. We monitor rollout status using kubectl rollout status. 
If any issue occurs, we quickly revert to the previous stable version using kubectl rollout undo.

   stage('Deploy') {
     steps {
       sh '''
       kubectl set image deployment/healthcare-app \
       healthcare-container=myrepo/healthcare-app:$BUILD_NUMBER
       '''
     }
   }
   stage('Verify Deployment') {
     steps {
       sh 'kubectl rollout status deployment/healthcare-app'
     }
   }
   stage('Rollback if Failed') {
     steps {
       sh 'kubectl rollout undo deployment/healthcare-app'
     }
   }


16. Deployment Strategy - Blue Green Deployment

Blue/Green Deployment is a strategy where you have two identical environments: one is the live environment (Blue) 
serving all production traffic, and the other is the new version (Green). You deploy the new application version to the Green environment, 
test it thoroughly, and when it’s verified, you switch the traffic from Blue to Green. This allows zero-downtime deployment and an easy rollback 
if anything goes wrong.
Example:
Suppose we have a web application running in Kubernetes on EKS. The Blue environment is currently serving users. 
We build a new version of the application and deploy it to the Green environment, which is fully isolated from Blue. 
Once we verify that the Green environment is stable and passing all tests, we update the service or load balancer to route 100% of traffic to Green. 
If a problem occurs, we can instantly switch back to Blue.


17. What is Autoscaling and Autohealing in K8s

In Kubernetes, autoscaling automatically adjusts resources based on demand. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler scales pods up or down 
based on metrics like CPU, and Cluster Autoscaler adds or removes nodes if needed. For example, if traffic spikes, more pods are 
created automatically. Autohealing ensures high availability by detecting unhealthy pods using liveness and readiness probes; if a pod fails, 
Kubernetes automatically replaces it with a new one. Together, autoscaling and autohealing keep applications resilient and performant without manual intervention.


18. What is the Services in K8s.
a Service provides a stable network endpoint to access a set of pods, 
even as pods are created or destroyed. It acts as a load balancer inside the cluster and ensures reliable communication. 
There are different types: ClusterIP for internal access, NodePort to expose pods on node ports, and LoadBalancer to create an external load balancer. 
For example :  a web app with multiple pods can be accessed by other services or users through a LoadBalancer Service, without worrying about individual pod IPs


19. Pods Continous failing-Troubleshoot
If a pod is continuously failing, I first check its status using 
kubectl get pods and inspect events with kubectl describe pod to understand why it’s failing, s
uch as image pull errors, resource limits, or probe failures. Then I check container logs with kubectl logs to see 
application-specific errors and verify environment variables, secrets, and mounted volumes. I also ensure the node has enough resources 
and no taints preventing scheduling. Once the root cause is identified, I fix the configuration or deployment and redeploy the pod, 
monitoring it to confirm it runs successfully.

